===== sales:value (81s, 12421 chars)
The Margin Blind Spot Audit is Zander's free 30-minute session for MDs of M&E and HVAC contracting firms. The owner walks away knowing exactly where their margin is most likely bleeding, whether their retentions position is a crisis or just messy, and what one thing to change this week before the next final account lands short.

- **Name:** The Margin Blind Spot Audit
- **Length:** 30 minutes, no prep required from the owner
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over between £2m and £12m, winning work but not banking what they expected
- **What they get:** A straight look at where margin is most likely going on live schemes, a quick read on the retentions position, and one or two things they can act on before the next final account lands
- **The promise:** In 30 minutes you will know more about where your profit is going than most management accounts will tell you in six months. No pitch. No fluff. No catch.

### The questions

| Area | Question | Red looks like | Green looks like |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Job costing visibility | On a scheme running right now, can you tell me what the margin is looking like at this point in the programme? | Owner relies on the final account to find out. No live numbers during the job. | Owner can pull up a cost-to-complete within 24 hours and the contracts manager reviews it weekly. |
| Margin bleed on finished jobs | Think of a job in the last 18 months that came back short on the final account. Could you explain at the time where the gap was? | Owner suspects a few reasons but cannot pin the gap to specific lines. Same story repeats on similar schemes. | Owner can point to the exact cause: absorbed variations, underpriced second fix, programme squeeze. It was caught early enough to recover some of it. |
| Variation control | When a variation comes up on site, who decides whether to absorb it or claim it back, and how quickly does that happen? | Site supervisor or engineer absorbs it on the day. It surfaces weeks later, if at all. The value is gone. | There is a clear rule. Variations above a set value go to the contracts manager the same week. Applications are updated before the next valuation. |
| Retentions | How much money is sitting in retentions right now across all your contracts, and who is responsible for chasing it? | Owner has a rough figure but no schedule. Nobody owns the chase. Some older ones have been written off informally. | There is a live retentions log. One person is accountable. Each holdback has a release date and a named action. |
| Applications and cash flow | How often do applications go in late, and what happens when the MC queries one? | Applications slip by a few days regularly. Queries sit in someone's inbox. Cash arrives later than it should. | Applications go in on time every cycle without the MD chasing. Queries are turned around within 48 hours. |
| Owner dependency | On your last break of four or more days, how many times did the business call you for a decision? | Phone did not stop. Owner made calls from the car park, the kitchen, wherever they were. The break was not a break. | Two calls or fewer. Both were genuinely exceptional. The contracts manager handled the rest. |
| Management information | When do your management accounts arrive, and by the time you see them, can you still change anything they describe? | Accounts arrive five or six weeks late. They describe history. Nothing on a live scheme can be changed using them. | Owner has a weekly numbers summary on live jobs. Management accounts are used to confirm what the owner already knows. |
| Pricing confidence | When you put a price in on a scheme similar to ones you have done before, how confident are you that the margin in the tender reflects what actually lands on the final account? | Owner knows there is usually a gap but cannot identify where in the pricing it happens. Tenders are based on feel as much as cost. | Owner can trace the margin from tender to final account on at least three recent jobs. Pricing is adjusted based on what previous schemes actually cost. |
| Key person risk | If your contracts manager handed in their notice tomorrow, what operational knowledge walks out with them? | Most of what makes the operation run is in one or two people's heads. No handover pack exists. Loss of that person is a crisis. | Key processes are written down or systematic enough that a replacement could be brought up to speed within a few weeks without the MD covering the gap personally. |

### Your opening questions

1. What is the biggest thing in the business right now that is stopping you banking what you should be from the work you are winning? I am not talking about the order book or the pipeline - I mean once the jobs are done, what is getting in the way of the money being where it should be?
2. If we were sitting here twelve months from now and things had gone the way you want them to, what would be different? What would you be able to tell me about how the business is running and what you are taking home?
3. Why does getting that sorted actually matter to you, beyond just the numbers? Because most MDs I talk to have a reason behind the reason - whether that is family time, being able to step back eventually, or just not wanting to feel like the business is running them rather than the other way round.
4. What have you already had a go at to get on top of this - spreadsheets, bringing someone in, promoting someone internally? Tell me what you tried and how that landed, because I want to work with where you are, not start from scratch.

### The magic question

**The magic question**

```text
Based on what you have told me, I think I know where the problem is sitting. Would it be useful if I showed you how I help MDs in exactly this position get on top of it - and what that typically looks like in practice?
```

_Wait for the yes. Do not move until you have it._

**Bridge into the offer**

```text
Right. So you have told me that the Wakefield-type jobs are landing short and you cannot fully explain the gap before the final account tells you it is too late. You want to be in a position twelve months from now where you know at week four whether a scheme is making money, and you can take a week off without the phone going. And underneath that, you want to know the twenty years you have put into this is actually building something worth having.

Here is how I typically work with clients who are in that spot.

First, I get properly into the numbers. In the first few weeks I sit in on job reviews and pricing discussions. Not to observe - to dig. I want to see where the margin is going on live schemes while there is still time to do something about it. That is not something an accountant does, and it is not something a consultant does after writing a report. It is hands-on, and it starts week one.

Second, I build a weekly rhythm around what is actually running. Not last month's management accounts. Live job costing, retentions tracking, and a clear picture of where each scheme is heading before the final account lands. You stop flying blind.

Third, I work on getting you out of the operational middle. That means the contracts manager and the site team making their own calls on the day-to-day, with clear lines so the right things still get to you and the wrong ones do not. The goal is that a week off is actually a week off.

I carried this same operation at Harlow & Pike - we grew the HVAC division from £4.2m to £11.8m in five years by fixing exactly these things. I know how the mechanics work because I have lived them.

Does that sound like the kind of support that would make a difference for you?
```

_Reflect back the owner's own words from the opening questions. Tie each of the three points to their specific challenge, their 12-month outcome, and their personal reason. Add the Harlow & Pike proof before the close. Pause after the final question._

