```

_Post on a Monday morning when MDs are back at their desk after a weekend of thinking. End with a direct invitation to message, not a link. This post invites conversation, not clicks._

> **Posting rhythm:** Four posts a month is the floor, not the target. One proof or career post, one pain post built around a 3am thought, one practical post on margin or retentions or owner dependency, and one question or opinion post that invites MDs to reply. Post on Tuesday or Wednesday mornings when your buyers are at their desks. Put calls to action in the first comment, not the post itself, so the algorithm does not limit reach. Reply to every comment the same day - conversation in the comments is worth more than extra posts.

===== content:newsletter (37s, 4937 chars)
The Contractor's Profit Letter is a short, practical read for MDs and owners of M&E and HVAC contracting firms who are winning work but not banking what they expected. Each issue covers one idea, one example, and one thing to act on this week.

- **Name:** The Contractor's Profit Letter
- **Who it is for:** MDs and owners of M&E, HVAC and electrical contracting firms turning over £2m to £12m
- **How often:** Monthly
- **Format:** Email and LinkedIn newsletter
- **Call to action:** Book a free 45-minute profit visibility session with Zander on a Monday or Thursday from 1pm

### The first issue

**Issue 1**

```text
Subject: The Wakefield job looked solid. So why is the final account short?

Every M&E MD I speak to has a version of the same story.

The job was priced right. The lads were on it. Applications went in on time. Then the final account lands and there is a forty or fifty grand gap, and nobody in the business can fully explain it.

I lived this at Harlow & Pike. We had schemes that looked fine at tender, ran smoothly enough on site, and still came back short. The problem was not that we were bad at the work. The problem was that by the time the final account told us what had happened, there was nothing left to do about it on that job or the next one.

Here is what we found was causing the gap, more often than anything else.

First, variations getting absorbed without being claimed. The programme gets squeezed, the scope shifts, the engineers crack on because that is what they do, and nobody gets the paperwork in before the window closes.

Second, the second fix being underpriced at tender and nobody catching it mid-job because there is no weekly look at where the cost is sitting against the estimate.

Third, the application going in on time but the supporting detail being thin, so the MC picks holes in it and knocks it back, and the argument never gets picked up properly.

None of those are disasters on their own. Together, they are forty grand you will never see.

The fix is not complicated. It is a weekly look at live job cost against estimate while the scheme is still running. Not a full audit. Not a new system. Just someone asking the right questions at week four, when there is still time to do something about it.

One thing to do this week: pick one live scheme and ask your contracts manager where the cost is sitting against estimate right now. Not at the end of the job. Now. If they cannot answer without going away for two days, you have found the problem.

If you want to get proper visibility on where the margin is going across your current order book, I offer a free 45-minute session to MDs who want a straight conversation about it. No pitch. Just a look at what is happening on your jobs and what is worth tackling first.

Book a time on a Monday or Thursday from 1pm: [booking link]

Zander Demo
The Contractor's Profit Practice
[email] | [phone]
```

### The next six issues

| Issue | Subject line | The one idea |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 2 | There is money sat out there from jobs you finished eighteen months ago | Retentions do not chase themselves. One person, one schedule, one chase call a week is the difference between banking the money and writing it off. This issue sets out the simplest retention tracking system that actually gets used. |
| 3 | Your management accounts are six weeks old. Here is what to look at instead | Last month's numbers describe what already happened. A weekly look at live job cost, application status and retention position tells you what is happening now. This issue explains what a weekly numbers rhythm looks like in a contracting business. |
| 4 | Dave's good. So why is he still ringing you? | Promoting a senior engineer or contracts manager does not automatically move the decisions down. This issue covers how to set the boundaries properly so the calls stop coming through to the MD without the business unravelling in the process. |
| 5 | You priced that job to make fifteen percent. Where did it go? | Margin bleed has three common causes in M&E contracting: unpriced variations, second fix underestimated at tender, and applications that do not get pushed back hard enough. This issue covers how to spot which one is costing you most before the next final account lands. |
| 6 | What is this business actually worth if you are not in it? | Twenty years of work built into a business that only runs because the owner is in the middle of it has a sale price to match. This issue explains what buyers look for in a contracting business and what that means for how you run it today. |
| 7 | The scheme that felt wrong from the start, and how to spot the next one earlier | Some jobs bleed margin from day one because of how they were priced, what was left out of the tender, or who the client is. This issue covers the signals to look for before you commit, and how to price the risk in rather than absorbing it at the end. |

===== ninety:quarter (38s, 4971 chars)
One objective for the next 90 days: sign your first three to four clients, build the weekly rhythm that proves the practice works, and have a pipeline that makes eight clients inevitable.

- **£7,800/mo** Income at four clients (minimum fee of £1,950 each)
- **4 clients** Target by week 12 to prove the model and review pricing
- **12 calls** Prospect calls needed to sign four clients, assuming one in three converts
- **60+ replies** Conversations needed to book 12 calls, assuming one in five replies books

> **On the income target:** These figures are built from Zander's actual minimum fee of £1,950 and a capacity of eight. The quarter target is four clients, not six, because the early weeks carry setup time and the sector moves at its own pace. Four clients at £1,950 is £7,800 a month and a solid proof-of-concept. Pricing review happens after client two or three.

### Week by week

- **Weeks 1 to 2:** Foundations locked. LinkedIn rewritten and published. Profit Coach page live with the offer, the Harlow & Pike proof, and the BOSS Scorecard link. Practice email active. Calendar connected with prospect call slots (Mondays and Thursdays from 1pm). Prospect list of 100 warm contacts and 900 targeted MDs in HVAC, M&E and electrical contracting compiled. First outreach message drafted and tested on five contacts before the campaign goes wide.
- **Weeks 2 to 4:** First campaign live. LinkedIn connection requests sending daily to MDs of M&E and HVAC firms in West Yorkshire, South Yorkshire and Greater Manchester. Target: 50 requests a week, 20 accepted, five replies. Follow-up messages sent within 24 hours of acceptance. Every reply handled the same day. Goal by end of week four: two to three prospect calls booked.
- **Weeks 4 to 8:** Calls running, first client signed. Two to three prospect calls a week. Value sessions run for the strongest prospects: Zander gets into their numbers, calls the margin problem, and shows what the weekly rhythm looks like in practice. First client signed by week six. Content goes out four times a month on LinkedIn: job costing, retentions, final accounts, and the cost of waiting. Each post draws on the Harlow & Pike experience directly.
- **Weeks 8 to 12:** Second campaign angle tested, referrals asked for. Second outreach angle tested: retention cash and cash flow pressure rather than margin bleed as the lead hook. Pricing reviewed after clients two and three. First newsletter issue sent to the prospect list. Referrals asked for from the first client once early results are visible. Pipeline target: eight to ten live conversations at any point, enough to make the next client a matter of when, not if.

### Weekly numbers

| Activity | Per week |
| --- | --- |
| LinkedIn connection requests sent | 50 |
| Connections accepted (target) | 20 |
| Follow-up messages sent to accepted connections | 20 |
| Replies received (target) | 5 |
| Prospect calls booked from replies | 1 to 2 |
| Prospect calls run | 1 to 2 |
| Value sessions run | 1 (from week 5) |
| LinkedIn posts published | 1 |
| Prospect list contacts added | 50 |

### This week

1. **Get the foundations in place.** LinkedIn rewritten and live. Profit Coach page checked: offer, proof, BOSS Scorecard link, and booking link all working. Practice email set up. Calendar open for prospect calls on Monday and Thursday afternoons. Do not start outreach until someone who clicks your profile lands somewhere credible.
2. **Build the prospect list.** Pull 100 contacts to start: former colleagues, subcontractors, engineers, MCs, anyone from fifteen years in building services who is now an MD or owner. Then use LinkedIn search to build toward 1,000 targeted MDs in HVAC, M&E and electrical contracting, £2m to £12m businesses, West Yorkshire outward first. This is the pipeline. Without it, nothing else runs.
3. **Write and test the first outreach message.** Draft the connection request and the follow-up. Lead with the margin bleed problem and the Harlow & Pike proof. Test on five warm contacts before the campaign goes wide. Read every reply the same day and respond within 24 hours. The goal this week is not a signed client. It is the first real conversation.

> **The most likely constraint: getting pulled into delivery before the pipeline is full:** Zander's strength is also his risk. He gets into the numbers from week one, and the first client will feel absorbing. In M&E and HVAC, the work is specific and the early sessions with a new client are intense. The danger is that outreach stops the moment client work starts, the pipeline dries up, and weeks eight to twelve become a scramble to restart from scratch. Spot it early: if you go two weeks without sending connection requests because you were busy with a client, that is the warning sign. Protect Monday and Thursday afternoons for prospect calls and outreach regardless of what is happening on the client side. The practice only compounds if the pipeline keeps moving.
